

## SQL Data Types (Box Format)

    
• String Data types
• Numeric Data types
• Date and time Data types
• Boolean Data Types
• Binary Data Types

    ### 1. Numeric Data Types
        
        | Data Type    | Description                         | Example   |
        | ------------ | ----------------------------------- | --------- |
        | INT          | Stores whole numbers                | 10, 25    |
        | SMALLINT     | Smaller range integers              | 100       |
        | BIGINT       | Very large integers                 | 987654321 |
        | DECIMAL(p,s) | Exact decimal values                | 45000.75  |
        | NUMERIC(p,s) | Same as DECIMAL                     | 99.99     |
        | FLOAT        | Approximate decimal values          | 4.5       |
        | REAL         | Approximate values (less precision) | 3.14      |
        | DOUBLE       | High precision floating point       | 123.456   |
        
        ---
        
    ### 2. Character / String Data Types
        
        | Data Type  | Description          | Example |
        | ---------- | -------------------- | ------- |
        | CHAR(n)    | Fixed-length text    | 'M'     |
        | VARCHAR(n) | Variable-length text | 'Amit'  |
        | TEXT       | Large text data      | Address |
        
        ---
        
    ### 3. Date & Time Data Types
        
        | Data Type | Description               | Example          |
        | --------- | ------------------------- | ---------------- |
        | DATE      | Stores date               | 2026-02-04       |
        | TIME      | Stores time               | 10:30:00         |
        | DATETIME  | Date and time             | 2026-02-04 10:30 |
        | TIMESTAMP | Date & time with timezone | Auto-generated   |
        
        ---
        
    ### 4. Boolean Data Types
        
        | Data Type | Description   | Example      |
        | --------- | ------------- | ------------ |
        | BOOLEAN   | True or False | TRUE / FALSE |
        | BIT       | Binary value  | 1 / 0        |
        
        ---
        
    ### 5. Binary Data Types
        
        | Data Type | Description              | Example    |
        | --------- | ------------------------ | ---------- |
        | BLOB      | Binary Large Object      | Image, PDF |
        | BINARY    | Fixed-length binary data | File data  |
        | VARBINARY | Variable-length binary   | Audio      |


   Differences between FLOAT and DECIMAL

    ### DECIMAL(p, s)
        * Stores **exact numeric values**
        * `p` = total number of digits
        * `s` = number of digits after the decimal point
        * No rounding errors
        * Best for **financial and monetary data**
        **Example**
        salary DECIMAL(10,2)
        12345.67
        1000.00
    ### FLOAT
        * Stores **approximate numeric values**
        * May cause small rounding errors
        * Uses less storage
        * Best for **scientific calculations** or approximate values
        **Example**
        rating FLOAT
        4.6999999 instead of 4.7
    
   Difference betwenn char and varchar

        | Feature     | CHAR                    | VARCHAR                   |
    | ----------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------- |
    | Length      | Fixed                   | Variable                  |
    | Storage     | Uses full length        | Uses only required length |
    | Space usage | More                    | Less                      |
    | Performance | Faster                  | Slightly slower           |
    | Best for    | Fixed values (Y/N, M/F) | Names, addresses          |


